### Steps to reproduce

1. `flutter create --platforms web --empty slider_repro && cd slider_repro`
2. Replace `lib/main.dart` with the code sample below. It has two sliders: A is enabled from the first frame, B starts disabled (`onChanged: null`) and is enabled by a button.
3. `flutter build web --release`, serve `build/web` (for example `python3 -m http.server 8765` in that folder) and open it in Chrome.
4. Press "Enable B" (the app calls `ensureSemantics()`, so the semantics tree is already on).
5. In DevTools, read the hidden range inputs:

```js
[...document.querySelectorAll('flt-semantics-host input[type="range"]')]
  .map((e) => [e.getAttribute('aria-valuetext'), e.value, e.min, e.max])
```

### Expected results

Slider B's input carries `aria-valuetext="B within 5 km"` (what `semanticFormatterCallback` returns) as soon as it is enabled, like slider A does from the first frame.

### Actual results

Measured with Playwright on the release build:

| | `aria-valuetext` | `value` | `min` | `max` |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| A, first frame | `A within 5 km` | `1` | `0` | `2` |
| B, after enabling | missing | `50` | empty | empty |

B's input exists only after it is enabled, and it never gets its attributes: no `aria-valuetext`, and the browser's default range value of 50 out of 0 to 100, which is all assistive technology has to announce. The attributes appear only after B's value changes once.

In our app the distance slider of a filter sheet starts disabled behind an "Anywhere" switch, so a screen-reader user who turns that switch off hears "50" instead of the distance.

### Likely cause

From reading `lib/web_ui/lib/src/engine/semantics/incrementable.dart` (unchanged in this respect on `master` at f1a05499eb, 2026-10-02):

- Enabling the slider gives its existing semantics node the increase and decrease actions, so a new `SemanticIncrementable` is created for a node whose `value`, `increasedValue` and `decreasedValue` were set in an earlier update.
- `_pendingResync` starts as `false`, and none of those fields is dirty in that update, so `_updateInputValues()` returns early and never writes `value`, `min`, `max` or `aria-valuetext` to the new `<input>`.

#187217 (the fix for #186472, in 3.47.0) made `update()` call `_updateInputValues()` in every gesture mode, but this dirty check still skips a role created for an already-populated node. A possible fix, untested: start `_pendingResync` as `true`, so a role's first update always syncs the input.

### Code sample

<details open><summary>Code sample</summary>

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:flutter/semantics.dart';

void main() {
  WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();
  SemanticsBinding.instance.ensureSemantics();
  runApp(const MaterialApp(home: Demo()));
}

class Demo extends StatefulWidget {
  const Demo({super.key});
  @override
  State<Demo> createState() => _DemoState();
}

class _DemoState extends State<Demo> {
  double a = 5;
  double b = 5;
  bool bEnabled = false;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => Scaffold(
    body: Column(
      mainAxisAlignment: MainAxisAlignment.center,
      children: [
        // Enabled from the first frame.
        Slider(
          min: 1,
          max: 20,
          value: a,
          semanticFormatterCallback: (v) => 'A within ${v.round()} km',
          onChanged: (v) => setState(() => a = v),
        ),
        // Starts disabled, enabled by the button below.
        Slider(
          min: 1,
          max: 20,
          value: b,
          semanticFormatterCallback: (v) => 'B within ${v.round()} km',
          onChanged: bEnabled ? (v) => setState(() => b = v) : null,
        ),
        ElevatedButton(
          onPressed: () => setState(() => bEnabled = true),
          child: const Text('Enable B'),
        ),
      ],
    ),
  );
}
```

</details>

