### Steps to reproduce

1. Use a window with a hinge `DisplayFeature` and a side safe area, e.g. a foldable held as a book with the status bar on the right (iPhone Duo simulator, Xcode 27.1, iOS 27.1, inner display 951 × 669 pt).
2. Call `showModalBottomSheet(useSafeArea: true, anchorPoint: …)` with an anchor on the left sub-screen.
3. Run the test below.

### Expected results

The sheet fills the left sub-screen: 455.5 pt wide. That sub-screen does not touch the right safe area, so nothing should be subtracted.

### Actual results

The sheet is 375.5 pt wide. The right inset (80 pt) of the window is subtracted from the left sub-screen as well. With `useSafeArea: false` the width is 455.5 pt.

In the iPhone Duo simulator (Xcode 27.1 Device Hub, book posture, status bar on the right), a left sheet is about 372 pt wide instead of 455 pt. A dimmed strip is left between the sheet and the hinge.

### Cause

`ModalBottomSheetRoute.buildPage` wraps the `DisplayFeatureSubScreen` in the `SafeArea`:

```dart
final Widget content = DisplayFeatureSubScreen(anchorPoint: anchorPoint, child: …);
Widget bottomSheet = useSafeArea
    ? SafeArea(bottom: false, child: content)
    : MediaQuery.removePadding(context: context, removeTop: true, child: content);
```

`SafeArea` shrinks the constraints but keeps `MediaQuery.size`. `DisplayFeatureSubScreen` therefore pads to the sub-screen in full-window coordinates (`right: parentSize.width - subScreen.right`) inside a box that is already narrower by the inset.

`RawDialogRoute` (`showDialog`) applies its `SafeArea` inside `DisplayFeatureSubScreen`. There, `MediaQueryData.removeDisplayFeatures` already gives each sub-screen only the padding of its own edges. Putting the sheet's `SafeArea` inside the sub-screen in the same way would fix this.

