### Steps to reproduce

### Steps to reproduce

1. On an Intel Mac with a discrete AMD GPU (tested: iMac 27" 2019, Radeon Pro 570X), run a macOS app with Impeller (default) on the master channel.
2. Read back the pixels of any `ui.Image` with `toByteData` (rawRgba or rawStraightRgba).
3. Compare with the same app on a Mac where Flutter runs on an integrated GPU.



### Expected results

`toByteData` returns the image's pixels.

### Actual results

On the Radeon Pro 570X, every byte is 0. This happens for every kind of image tested:

| Image | Size | rawRgba | rawStraightRgba |
|---|---|---|---|
| JPG decoded with `instantiateImageCodec` | 1092x1092 | 0.0% non-zero bytes | 0.0% non-zero bytes |
| `PictureRecorder` → `toImage` (red rect) | 64x64 | 0.0% | 0.0% |
| `decodeImageFromPixels` (red pixels) | 64x64 | 0.0% | 0.0% |

The same build on a MacBook Pro (Intel, Flutter running on the integrated GPU) returns the correct pixels.
The images draw correctly on screen; only the CPU readback is zero. The same happens with `RenderRepaintBoundary.toImage` + `toByteData` (screenshots of a visible scene come back black).

This breaks every package that reads textures back on the CPU. In flutter_scene, every texture loaded from an asset or a .glb is read back before upload, so all textured 3D content renders black on this machine (https://github.com/bdero/flutter_scene/issues/509). Textures uploaded directly from pixels (`Texture2D.fromPixels`) render fine, and a Unity build of the same scene renders correctly on the same iMac, so the GPU and driver are fine.

Cause (diagnosed by @bdero in flutter_scene#509): on Macs without unified memory, Impeller allocates the readback buffer with `MTLStorageModeManaged`, and the texture-to-buffer blit in `BlitPassMTL::OnCopyTextureToBufferCommand` never calls `synchronizeResource:` on it, so the CPU reads stale memory. Apple Silicon and Intel integrated GPUs use shared storage, which is why only discrete GPUs are affected.

### Code sample

```dart
import 'dart:async';
import 'dart:typed_data';
import 'dart:ui' as ui;

import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

Future<void> main() async {
  WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();

  Future<void> report(String what, ui.Image image) async {
    final b = (await image.toByteData(format: ui.ImageByteFormat.rawRgba))!
        .buffer
        .asUint8List();
    final nonZero = b.where((v) => v != 0).length;
    debugPrint('$what ${image.width}x${image.height}: '
        '${(100 * nonZero / b.length).toStringAsFixed(1)}% non-zero bytes, '
        'first pixel ${b.take(4).toList()}');
  }

  // 1. Picture -> Image
  final recorder = ui.PictureRecorder();
  ui.Canvas(recorder).drawRect(const Rect.fromLTWH(0, 0, 64, 64),
      Paint()..color = const Color(0xFFFF0000));
  await report('picture', await recorder.endRecording().toImage(64, 64));

  // 2. Pixels -> Image
  final px = Uint8List(64 * 64 * 4);
  for (var i = 0; i < px.length; i += 4) {
    px[i] = 255;
    px[i + 3] = 255;
  }
  final c = Completer<ui.Image>();
  ui.decodeImageFromPixels(px, 64, 64, ui.PixelFormat.rgba8888, c.complete);
  await report('pixels', await c.future);

  runApp(const SizedBox());
}
```

Expected output: `100.0% non-zero bytes, first pixel [255, 0, 0, 255]` for both.
Output on the Radeon Pro 570X: `0.0% non-zero bytes, first pixel [0, 0, 0, 0]` for both.

